{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "phase_shift",
      "parent_relation": "The restoring acceleration depends on eta and the lagged compression surrogate only, so for a given observed eta and depth_compression the stiffness correction is identical whether the surface excursion is growing or receding.",
      "child_relation": "The finite-amplitude restoring correction will also depend on the sign and magnitude of the local excursion-rate state, creating a rate-dependent phase lag in effective stiffness so the same eta can produce different du_dt during advancing versus retreating motion.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "eta",
        "u",
        "depth_compression"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At matched eta and similar depth_compression, the acceleration becomes systematically different between portions of the cycle with positive versus negative excursion rate, producing asymmetric looping in du_dt against eta that the parent cannot generate."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "restoring_core",
        "expression": "gravity * wavenumber * eta",
        "role": "Baseline hydrostatic standing-wave restoring acceleration scale"
      },
      {
        "name": "compression_fraction",
        "expression": "depth_compression / depth_saturation_scale**2",
        "role": "Normalized finite-depth geometric compression level already used to stiffen and amplify drag"
      },
      {
        "name": "velocity_ratio",
        "expression": "Abs(u) / velocity_transition_scale",
        "role": "Dimensionless wave-speed regime indicating when finite-rate effects are dynamically noticeable within a cycle"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when excursions are large enough that compression_fraction is non-negligible and the standing wave is not overdamped, so u remains appreciable during a cycle; then finite-depth geometry changes quickly enough that restoring response can differ between advancing and receding phases.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training the effect appears as modest within-cycle hysteresis once both compression_fraction and velocity_ratio reach order-unity influence; in holdout at somewhat larger but still bounded amplitudes the same mechanism strengthens the phase-dependent stiffness difference rather than introducing a different process.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the limit of vanishing rate-dependent geometric lag, equivalently when the added excursion-rate influence is set to zero or when motion is slow compared with geometric relaxation so restoring stiffness depends only on eta through depth_compression.",
    "freedom_change": "add_state",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "A distinct excursion-rate or memory state is needed because eta, u, and the existing depth_compression alone do not separately encode whether finite-depth geometry is currently building or relaxing at the same observed displacement. Without an added state, the target mapping remains single-phase in the observed variables.",
    "gap_statement": "The current model assumes finite-amplitude hydrostatic stiffening is quasi-static apart from the compression surrogate magnitude, omitting any dynamical lag of the free-surface geometry relative to the standing-wave excursion.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Finite-depth restoring correction is determined only by instantaneous displacement filtered through one magnitude-like compression surrogate, with no phase-dependent geometric memory beyond that scalar occupancy.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "A weak dynamic free-surface shape/pressure-adjustment lag can make the effective finite-amplitude restoring stiffness depend on whether the excursion is increasing or decreasing, while remaining unforced and bounded.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "eta",
      "u",
      "depth_compression"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the quasi-static assumption behind the nonlinear restoring correction. The parent already includes finite-amplitude stiffening, but it uses a single compression surrogate that depends only on excursion magnitude and relaxes monotonically toward a symmetric geometric target. Thus, conditional on the observed states eta and depth_compression, the restoring part of du_dt has one fixed phase relation: the same eta and depth_compression imply the same restoring correction regardless of whether the surface is moving toward or away from crest. Vary the oscillation amplitude so that finite-depth effects are visible, while holding gravity, wavenumber, and damping parameters fixed and keeping the same observation protocol over time. In that admissible regime, a child model with an added rate-memory state can produce a measurable contrast: during rapid excursion growth versus decay at similar eta, the effective stiffness differs, changing the local slope/loop structure of du_dt versus eta. A single refitted parent parameter set cannot reproduce this phase-dependent contrast across those conditions because its restoring mapping is single-valued in the supplied states and lacks any extra coordinate that distinguishes advancing from receding geometry. The refinement remains scientifically admissible because it modifies internal pressure-geometry adjustment without adding forcing, and can be taken weak so dissipation still dominates net energy decay."
  },
